## KEYWORDS
ZIF-8, facet effect, single atom catalyst, oxygen reduction reaction
## 1 Introduction
Single atom catalysts (SACs) derived from metal organic frameworks (MOFs) have drawn tremendous attention [l-3], because the high specific surface area and unique micropores matrix of MOFs expose large number of active sites that endows SACs with high catalytic properties [4-7]. Moreover, single atomic sites in such catalysts also possess predictable and flexible coordination structure. On the one hand, metal atoms occupy the Zn sites by the evaporation of $Z\mathrm{n}$ or maintain the original structure in MOFs by etching the excess particles during the synthesis [8-12]. On the other hand, the addition of heteroatoms (S, P, Se, B etc.) [13-l6] and the alteration of different MOFs [17-19] in the synthetic strategy can largely affect the coordinate sites or the carrier of SACs. Thus, such catalysts have been widely studied in many field [20-25], especially in the field of electrocatalysis [26-30], such as oxygen reduction reaction (ORR) and oxygen evolution reaction (OER) [31-35], $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction reactions $(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR})$ [36],formic acid oxidations (FAOR) [37] et al. For example, Yuan et al. [38] chose carboxylate/amide mixedlinker MOF (DMOF) and ${\mathrm{CoCl}}_{2}{\cdot}6{\mathrm{H}}_{2}{\mathrm{O}}$ as precursors to synthesize Co@DMOF-900, which shows 8-fold higher turnover frequency $(2.015\mathrm{e}{\cdot}\mathrm{s}^{-1}$ site-) than Co SACs derived from ZIF-8; Jiang and coworkers [39] successfully synthesized $\mathrm{Fe_{1}/N\mathrm{-C}}$ by pyrolyzing PCN
222 with mixed ligand containing M-tetrakis((4-carboxyphenyl) porphyrin) (M-TCPP; $\mathbf{M}=\mathrm{Fe},$ Co, Ni and $\mathrm{Cu}^{\mathrm{\prime}}$ ）and $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ -TCPP, which can effectively reduce $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ under a pressure as low as 0.15 atm.
In recent years, several efforts have been employed to explore the morphology and facet effects of MOFs [40,41]. The orientation of micropores and the coordination number of surface metal sites are highly dependent on the exposed facets, resulting in the different catalytic performance. For example, Datta et al. [42] reported that AlFFIVE-Ni exposed with different facets possesses different orientation of micro channels and nanosheets with maximally exposed {0o1} facets exhibit the highest selectivity of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{S}$ and $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ in gas separation. Liu and his co-workers [43] devoted their efforts in the controllable synthesis of ZIF-8 with different morphology by using cetyltrimethylammonium bromide (CTAB) as capping agent and found that Zn species in {10o} facet have higher coordination number than that on {ll0} facet. Since single atomic sites usually maintain the original coordination structure of MOFs, the distinction of MOFs with various facets may also affect the electronic structure of finally formed active sites, which leads to the difference of catalytic properties of SACs. However, few works concentrated on the facet effect of MOFs derived SACs so far. Capping agents are always utilized in the morphology controllable synthesis of MOFs,which coordinates to the surface sites and leads to the different coordination structure of surface atomic sites. Therefore, the development of the thermodynamically controllable synthetic strategy to obtain MOFs with specific exposed facets under a uniform reaction system is of great significance for understanding the growth mechanism of MOFs and the facet effect on SACs derived from MOFs.
ZIF-8 shows a typical truncated rhombic dodecahedral morphology in methanol or DMF solution, but it changes to cube when N-cetyl-N,N,N-trimethylammonium bromide is used as the capping agent [44,45]. Herein, we successfully achieve the fabrication of ZIF-8 exposed with {100},{110},and mixed facet with different ratio by accurately controlled the synthesis temperature. Under $30~^{\circ}\mathrm{C},$ ZIF-8 grows along the {110} facet via an area-reducing layered growth mode to form truncated rhombic dodecahedral (ZIF-8-tr） and rhombic dodecahedral (ZIF-8-r) morphology successively. Surprisingly, when the synthetic system performs under a temperature as low as $-40\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C},$ the morphology change will disappear and only ZIF-8 nanocubes (ZIF-8-c) can be obtained by an integral layered growth mode. Moreover, the twogrowth process does not change in the presence of metal precursors $({\mathrm{Fe}}(\mathrm{acac})_{3},$ $\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{acac})_{2}$ and $\mathrm{Co}(\mathrm{acac})_{2})$ .Via same calcination process, single metal atom anchored on nitrogen doped carbon catalysts (SA-M/CN) are obtained by pyrolyzing corresponding metal immobilized ZIF-8 (M/ZIF-8) with different morphology and exposed facet. Extended X-ray absorption fine structure (EXAFS) spectra analysis reveals that the coordination number of Fe in SA-Fe-c is lower than that in SA-Fe-r and SA-Fe tr. Besides, SA-Fe/CNs also shows different ORR performance. The half-wave potential of SA-Fe/CN-c $(E_{1/2}=0.868\:\mathrm{V})$ is higher than both SA-Fe/CN-tr $(E_{1/2}=0.859\mathrm{~V})$ and SA-Fe/CN-r $(E_{1/2}=$ 0.851 V),indicating that the unsaturated coordination of single atom Fe boosts the catalytic performance of ORR.
## 2 Results and discussion
To complete the facet controllable synthesis strategy,we firstly explored the growth process of ZIF-8. When 2-methylimidazole and zinc nitrate were sonicated at room temperature for a short time ( $\mathrm{\Delta}5\mathrm{min})$ ), the monodispersed ZIF-8-c with cubic morphology dominated by $\{100\}$ facets can be observed in scanning electron microscopy (SEM） image (Fig. l(a)). However, ZIF-8-tr exposed with both $\{100\}$ and {llo} facets was observed by continuing stirring for 1 h (Fig.l(b)). These results illustrate that: 1) It is possible to synthesize cubic ZIF-8 without capping agent; 2) the ZIF-8-c can be used as crystal seeds during the synthetic process; 3) this reaction system can be utilized as a perfect model to deeply recognize the thermodynamically growth process.
Figure 2(a) exhibits a schematic illustration of the morphology controllable synthetic method involved in this study. At first, a solution containing 2-methylimidazole and zinc nitrate was sonicated under room temperature to form ZIF-8-c crystal seeds for $5\mathrm{min}$ The following morphology controllable growth of ZIF-8 are monitored under 30 or $-40{}^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ respectively for different times. When the reaction occurs at $30{}^{\circ}\mathrm{C},$ the cubic ZIF-8 crystals gradually grew into a truncated rhombic dodecahedral morphology. As shown in Fig.2(b), the average side length of cubic seeds is $203{\mathrm{nm}}$ After growth for $^{\textrm{1h},}$ we can see that oblate hexagons appear and the area of square shaped $\{100\}$ facet become smaller (Fig. 2(c)). With the increasing of the reaction times, the average length of the bevel edges ( $\dot{\mathbf{\Omega}}_{a}$ -edge) of oblate hexagons is extending and the side edge of the square ( $\mathbf{\bar{\theta}}$ edge) is shortening （ $^{\cdot}_{12\mathrm{h}}$ Fig.2(d)). Finally, four $a$ -edges intersect at a point to form a diamond face of rhombic dodecahedral $(24\ \mathrm{\textrm{h}};$ Fig.2(e). Moreover, the mean lengths of side edge of cubes or the diagonal lines (the dashed line) of oblate hexagon (or diamond) from Figs. 2(b)-2(e) have been measured and counted. We surprisingly discovered that the lengths show negligible changes during the growth process ( $203\mathrm{nm}$ in 5 min; $203{\mathrm{nm}}$ in 1 h; 205 nm in $12\mathrm{h};$ $205~\mathrm{nm}$ in $24\mathrm{~h~}$ ,even if the reaction time was extended to $96\mathrm{~h~}$ $206~\mathrm{nm}$ ； Fig.S2 in the ESM). These results strongly illustrate an area-reducing layered growth process is involved. During this growth process, zinc nitrate and 2-methylimidazole are only assembled on the {l00} surface of cubic seeds and the growth does not proceed on either corners or edges, which results in the reducing area of $\left\{100\right\}$ surface and forms the hexagonal or rhombic with $\{110\}$ facet. Interestingly, when the growth performed at $-40~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ with a similar size( $(193\ \mathrm{nm})$ ）of nanocube seeds (Fig. 2(f), no obvious morphology changes can be seen after a growth time of $72{\mathrm{~h}}.$ With the increasing of reaction time, the average side length of square from nanocubes became longer and longer ( $225\mathrm{nm}$ in $12\mathrm{{h}}.$ $264~\mathrm{nm}$ in $24\mathrm{h},$ $562\mathrm{nm}$ in $72\mathrm{{h}}$ ）andthe morphology maintained (Figs. $2(\mathrm{f}){-}2(\mathrm{i}),$ ，which indicate an integral layered growth at $-40\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}.$ During this process, the molecule cages not only fabricated on the {loo} surface, but also on the edges and corners, meaning that the new formed layer can cover the integral surface of ZIF-8 to form a larger cube.
Unlike metal nanoparticles with the isotropic metallic bond [46],ZIF-8 was formed with directivity by the coordinate bonds between Zn ions and 2-methylimidazole. Under a low temperature $(-40~^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ ， the pendant group coordinated with 2- methylimidazole and $Z\mathrm{n}$ ions identically to maintain the cubic morphology and form a kinetic product of ZIF-8-c. The $\{110\}$ facet with more saturated $Z\mathrm{n}$ sites decreases the surface energy and thus can be obtained via a higher temperature as the thermodynamic product.
